<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Hardened-runtime entitlements for Memoir.app. Used by Scripts/release.sh AND
    Scripts/build-app.sh. Both sign with --options runtime, so both need this file.

    CORRECTED 19 Aug 2026. This header used to say build-app.sh signed without entitlements
    "because a local ad-hoc build is not subject to the hardened runtime's resource gate."
    That is false. The gate belongs to --options runtime, which the dev build has always
    passed, and the note below about the failure being discoverable only from a notarised
    DMG was wrong for the same reason. It was found the ordinary way instead: "Read them
    now" reported that nothing had been granted, and the Photos pane in System Settings had
    no Memoir row to switch on, because a request that never reaches tccd never creates one.

    WHY THESE THREE, AND NOTHING ELSE
    =================================
    Under the hardened runtime, TCC refuses a protected resource outright when the binary
    does not carry the matching entitlement, and it refuses *silently*: the app never
    appears in the System Settings list for that resource, so the user sees a feature that
    does nothing rather than a permission they can grant. An Info.plist usage string alone
    is not enough. This is true of every build signed with --options runtime, the dev build
    included, and build-app.sh now verifies the keys survived signing rather than trusting
    that they were passed.

    The list below is not copied from a blog post. It is the set of keys that `tccd`, the
    daemon that actually enforces this, carries as strings:

        strings /System/Library/PrivateFrameworks/TCC.framework/Support/tccd \
            | grep '^com.apple.security.'

    which on macOS 15/26 yields exactly six recognised keys:

        com.apple.security.automation.apple-events
        com.apple.security.device.audio-input
        com.apple.security.device.camera
        com.apple.security.personal-information.addressbook
        com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars
        com.apple.security.personal-information.photos-library

    Memoir needs four of them. The other two (audio-input's sibling `device.camera`, and
    nothing else) it must not ask for.

    The fourth, photos-library, was added when the photo library became an import source. It is
    read for two fields per image, the date and the coordinate, and PhotoKit has no read-only
    access level: the only alternative to `.readWrite` is `.addOnly`, which grants writing and
    forbids reading, the exact inverse of what is wanted. So the system prompt necessarily asks
    for more than Memoir takes, and the onboarding and Settings copy both say what is actually
    read. `PhotoImporter` never calls PHImageManager, so no image reaches the memory; PhotoFrames does,
    to show today's photographs above the journal, and keeps none of them.

    DELIBERATELY ABSENT
    ===================
    com.apple.security.app-sandbox
        Never. The sandbox makes the accessibility tree of other processes unreadable, and
        reading that tree is the entire product. Adding this key does not degrade Memoir,
        it ends it.

    com.apple.security.accessibility
        Does not exist. Several widely-cited answers tell you to declare it for a hardened
        runtime; `tccd` has never heard of it (see the grep above: it is not in the six).
        It is a stale App Sandbox-era artefact. Accessibility is granted by the user in
        System Settings against NSAccessibilityUsageDescription and the code signature, and
        there is no entitlement to declare. Adding it would be cargo cult in a file whose
        whole job is to be exactly right.

    Reminders, and speech recognition
        Neither has an entitlement key of its own. EventKit's reminder store sits behind
        com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars (there is no
        ...personal-information.reminders in tccd's list, despite it turning up in write-ups
        of the sandbox). SFSpeechRecognizer is gated by NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription
        and nothing else.

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
        The usual reflex for a hardened runtime, and wrong here. Memoir has no third-party
        dependencies at all (Apple frameworks plus the system SQLite), so it never loads
        code Apple did not sign. Declaring it would weaken the runtime in exchange for
        nothing, and reviewers reasonably read it as a smell.

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit, ...allow-unsigned-executable-memory, ...debugger
        No JIT, no self-modifying code, no debugging in a shipped build.

    Screen Recording (CGPreflightScreenCaptureAccess, used for window titles) likewise has
    no entitlement: it is TCC-only, and Memoir degrades to app names without it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Reading which app is frontmost, so captured text can be attributed to the work it
         belongs to. Declared in Info.plist as NSAppleEventsUsageDescription. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Dictation into the ask bar, and only while the bar is open. Without this key the
         microphone is refused before the user is ever asked, and Memoir does not appear
         under Privacy & Security > Microphone at all. NSMicrophoneUsageDescription. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Two things sit behind this one key. Writing confirmed todos to Reminders via
         EventKit (reminders have no key of their own, as noted above) and reading the
         calendar during first-run import, which is where a decade of real people and real
         dates comes from on day one. NSRemindersFullAccessUsageDescription and
         NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription respectively. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Reading Contacts once, during first-run import, to learn the names of the people in
         the user's life. Without this key the notarised build does not merely fail to read
         them: Memoir never appears under Privacy & Security > Contacts at all, so the user
         is offered no way to grant it, the exact silent failure this file exists to prevent.
         Read-only and one-shot; nothing is ever written back to the address book.
         NSContactsUsageDescription. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.addressbook</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Reading the photo library. Two different things, kept apart on purpose. What is stored is
         metadata: creation date and coordinate per image, read by PhotoImporter, which does not
         call PHImageManager at all. What is shown is today's photographs, as thumbnails above the
         journal composer, fetched by PhotoFrames while that pane is open and kept nowhere.
         Screenshots are skipped by both. Without this key the notarised build never appears under
         Privacy & Security > Photos, so the user cannot grant it even if they want to.
         NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.photos-library</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
